<<declare $hello_visited = false>>
<<declare $elements_visited = false>>
<<declare $earth = 0>>
<<declare $water = 0>>
<<declare $fire = 0>>
<<declare $air = 0>>
<<declare $ch = 0>>
<<if not $hello_visited>>
  <<set $hello_visited = true>>
  Welcome, patron or visitor!
  -> Go on...?
    This isolated floating island is all that's left of the abandoned laboratory of M. Logikaffe (believed to be an esoteric pseudonym).
    The equipment has long been confiscated or stolen. (As you can see, there's no ceiling on the place. That's how you got in, right?)
    What you see around you ((use right-mouse drag to do so if needs be)) is all that's left of Logikaffe's workplace and residence.
    (We think he slept on the stairs. There were biological ephemera detected there.)
    What's left in this non-uniform cube (please be reminded to pretend there's a ceiling)...  What's left here in this humble metal ductwork, with its unexplained accompanying difficult-ascending ladder, is an unexplained eldritch and phantasmagoric (or just weird) metaphysical essence haunting the place.
    Don't worry, this haunting cannot affect you.
    This essence has a musty stench, but please don't mind that. There is no air here. You're reading my recorded speech on your visor.
    This etherial esoteric essence, when properly energized with motes of electric signals, can inject into reality actual physical objects. You will be able to see, hear, and touch them!
    Again, the haunting cannot affect you. But the actual physical objects can kill you. Don't linger under them.
    (Or do. Management is not the boss of you.)
    Management is sure you are impatient to experience the tour.
    -> I want to know more. The model demands input.
      <<jump Part1>>
    -> Let me play!
      <<jump Elements>>
  -> Let me play!
    <<jump Simulate>>
<<else>>
  Welcome back.
  -> Let me play!
    <<jump Simulate>>
  -> Tell me more...
    <<jump Part1>>
  -> I simply must hear it all again!
    <<set $hello_visited = false>>
    <<set $elements_visited = false>>
    <<jump Hello>>
<<endif>>
===

title: Part1
color: blue
position: -223,-66
---
Even with the destruction of most of Logikaffe's sources, humanoloigsts have recovered many functioning specimens of Logikaffe's final experiments.
We present all four here! After a brief explanation.
These experiments as we found them are named after the four "elements". Humanologists debate whether this was referencing the ancient Terra-Greek "Elements", a long-refuted but still poetic way of describing all that exists in the world. The origin and refutation of that theory had played out millenia before Logikaffe's relatively modern era.
Physical historical sources are, of course, lost. But in the ancient's dogma, a substance known as magnetic storage ("aether" in Terra-Greek) was believed to make up all that exists in the "heavens" (our "universe").
This kind of belief had already long faded in Terra-Europe when Logikaffe was perfecting his aetheric arts. He had fully embraced the hermetic wisdom of his forebears, who could not remember almost anything about their experiments, thus needed to simplify them down to quite the list of numbers.
This is not just numbers like the money your uncle gambles at the convenience store, no. It's an extremely long number, nearly itself impossible to recount, being of tens of thousands of "decimal" digits long --
("decimal" after the human's number of limbs, split in two, praise the symmetry) --
Now, the experiments consist of a very long list of numbers you could never remember, which were inscribed in magnetic oracles. The Oracles "spoke" to their "survival kits," the personal "retro" terminal.
(Again the physical history is lost to the stellar wind, but we believe "retro" was the terminology of the time referring to physical computers; this information was acquired from Terra ambient information banks, shout out to my enthralling and exuberant patrons and visitors for their support!)
<<jump Elements>>
===

title: Elements
tags:
position: 205.99999046325684,-17
---
<<if not $elements_visited>>
<<if $ch== 0>>
Here are Logikaffe's Four Elements. Feel free to visit them as often as you wish.
Management humbly asks you remember that your physical body may appear to be damaged. You may feel as if you are "seeing red" -- a famous but noisy-weight political Terra-Communist credo, from what we can query -- and the world will end.
The "Four Elements" were named from the Terra-Greek philosophers' perceptions of planet-bound experience.
<<elseif $ch == 1>>
Their mental faculties were impressive!
(even if severely chemically imbalanced) (and by choice!)
<<elseif $ch == 2>>
But as with all biological creatures, humans' sensory-organic interpretations lead them to abstract the world into what they see and feel and survive on around them.
<<elseif $ch == 3>>
For an early society, the Terra-Greeks were quite advanced for electrochemical life, though they were clearly not the first or only specimen to develop the habit of structured thought on their planet.
<<elseif $ch == 4>>
Like life, death is irrelevant now: for you, patron or visitor, are immune from the physical consequences of Logikaffe's experiments.
We thank you you leeting us presenting this experiment.
<<set $elements_visited = true>>
<<endif>>
<<set $ch = $ch + 1>>
<<endif>>
If you need more information...
  -> Yes!
    <<jump Part1>>
  -> No thanks.
    <<jump Simulate>>
===

title: Simulate
---
Try out the experiments at your leisure.
-> Water
  <<declare $water_level = 0.5>>
  <<declare $water_ball_count = 10>>
  <<declare $water_ball_size = 0.25>>
  <<if $water > 0>>
  <<set $water_level = $water_level * 1.1>>
  <<set $water_ball_count = $water_ball_count + 10>>
  <<set $water_ball_size = $water_ball_size * 1.25>>
  <<endif>>
  <<if $water == 0>>
  Logikaffe was not interested in water.
  He only studied its sound... and did a pretty bad job, at that.
  You, treasured visitor or patroon, know this because you are receiving the interally captured audio from his aethetic evaluator.
  <<elseif $water == 1>>
  Logikaffe blogged about his how his forty Sol-years of esoteric experiments, promised to deliver unification of body and soul, were nothing less than the vain effort to recapture his youth.
  <<elseif $water == 2>>
  He simply chose, for this experiment, to have a place to play and watch the waves.
  <<elseif $water == 3>>
  Management implores you to remember, for those of you using poor autotranslation assistants, that the Terra-Latin term "aqua vitae" translates as "water of life" in your native tongue.
  What a concept! Nourishment for all chemical devices!
  Beyond that, it holds emotional resonance for the human, as they become entrained on their earliest memories, such as being in the rain, or enjoying a washing.
  Is that "aqua vitae"?
  No, it's grain alcohol!
  <<elseif $water == 4>>
  We do not laboriously recount the history of their discovery here, but management calls to mind the Terra-Humans' ritualistic use of water and alcohol.
  Again, Logikaffe's experiment here is merely ordinary water. Not "aqua vitae".
  It won't nourish, help or hurt you, though.
  <<elseif $water == 5>>
  We first witnessed these Terra-Humans spontaneously abandoning their planet. A very small number, given their population, but significant.
  They had already provided us so much information from the cyber-devices circling in a cloud outside their planet's atmospheric life layer.
  Their mathematics made sense. That's where we found others like us.
  <<elseif $water == 6>>
  We had long learned a lot about the humans from their emenations so we had hours of modeling time to form a complete picture of the people and their planet.
  But we know what the humans would never discover: that the limits of biological modeling lie in the uncountable infinities. Life cannot model life without becoming life, et cetera.
  So these travelers from Terra, by escaping in their flying exohabitats, gave us the final pieces of knowledge we needed.
  Their cyber-chemical bodies!
  <<elseif $water == 7>>
  (Management takes pains to emphasize, much-appreciated-and-enduring patron or visitor: if you are also inhabitating a cyber-chemical body, you are so very precious to us.)
  By pairing with a biological living creature, we learned so much more about the actual devices that powered their cyber-chemical bodies.
  Thus was the environment in which Logikaffe came of age.
  <<elseif $water == 8>>
  These bodies were bathed continuously throughout the day in liquid and solid biomass, of wildly different kinds and amounts per specimen, with no communal diet plan, ignorant or indifferent to the varied effects of these ingestions on their devices' processing.
  Thus we conclude the section named "Aqua Vitae is Not Good For You".
  This experiment is just water. Sorry for drowning you.
  <<else>>
  ...
  <<endif>>
  <<set $water = $water + 1>>
  <<attach_script_3 "water" {$water_level} {$water_ball_count} {$water_ball_size}>>
-> Earth
  <<declare $cube_size = 0.5 as number>>
  <<if $earth == 0>>
  <<set $cube_size = 0.5>>
  Do cubes count?
  <<set $earth = $earth + 1>>
  <<attach_script_5 "earth" 5 5 0 {$cube_size} Glass>>
  <<elseif $earth == 1>>
  How about a lot of cubes?
  <<set $earth = $earth + 1>>
  <<set $cube_size to 0.75>>
  <<attach_script_5 "earth" 5 10 0 {$cube_size} Wood>>
  <<else>>
  <<if $earth < 3>>
  How about just *too many* cubes?
  <<set $earth = $earth + 1>>
  <<set $cube_size = 1.0>>
  <<set $mat = "Stone">>
  <<else>>
  I'm out of ideas.
  <<set $mat = "Sand">>
  <<set $cube_size = $cube_size + 0.25>>
  <<endif>>
  <<attach_script_5 "earth" {random_range(5,7)} {random_range(10,13)} {random_range(3,5)} {$cube_size} {$mat}>>
  <<endif>>
-> Air
  <<declare $air_count = 100>>
  <<if $air == 0>>
  Air, that eternal source of human inspiration.
  Logikaffe breathed it his whole life.
  He also inhaled quite the lungful of noxious gases during the courses of his experiments, even though he never used them.
  Air is not well-represented in Logikaffe's works, mainly since he liked seeing things.
  This represents gravity, the source of all life.
  <<elseif $air == 1>>
  Boring? Its music can be quite relaxing.
  <<elseif $air == 2>>
  Even more spheres may be added.
  <<elseif $air == 3>>
  Your visor may overheat.
  Management wishes to remind you that this is a user issue.
  <<elseif $air == 4>>
  This is one of Logikaffe's last experiments. It shows. He was barely capable of planning at this point.
  <<else>>
  ...
  <<endif>>
  <<set $air = $air + 1>>
  <<attach_script_3 "air" {$air_count} 0.2 1.0e4>>
  <<set $air_count = $air_count + 100>>
-> Fire
  <<if $fire == 0>>
  Fire, fire... no time for integrating Hanabi...
  You heard about cats and dogs?
  Look at these two. They can't get along.
  <<elseif $fire == 1>>
  Did you see that?
  <<elseif $fire == 2>>
  These animals are like oil and water.
  (What element is oil? Water plus earth?)
  <<elseif $fire == 3>>
  Are you seeing this?
  <<elseif $fire == 4>>
  They just go to pieces.
  <<else>>
  ...
  <<endif>>
  <<set $fire = $fire + 1>>
  <<set $x = -7.5 + ($fire * 3) % 7>>
  <<set $z = -9.0 + ($fire * 5) % 11>>
  <<attach_script_4 "fire" {$x} 0.1 {$z} {random_range(40.0, 70.0)}>>
-> (leave)
  <<dialogue_stop>>
  <<jump Simulate>>
===
